# M7 editor comparison, part 3 (tests/run_login_test.ps1): the player made by
# ds39_login_new.script edits through Dead Souls' own editor (lib/editor.c,
# a restricted ed session for a non-creator). `praise` opens it in input
# mode; the lines go in, then command mode (the "Command: " prompt), a
# print, a substitution, the help pager ("Help: "), an error, and x saves
# and runs the command's callback. `bug -r` opens it again, a `!` line runs a
# mud command from inside the editor, and `!quit` quits mid-edit, which
# lib/interactive.c handles with ed_cmd("."), ed_cmd("x"), ed_cmd("Q").
# The pauses keep under Dead Souls' 10-commands-a-second limit.
expect What name do you wish?
send alice
expect Password:
send secret12
expect sp:
pause 250
send praise
expect *
pause 250
send What a fine mud.
expect *
pause 250
send It edits well.
expect *
pause 250
send .
expect Command:
pause 250
send 1,$p
expect Command:
pause 250
send 1s/fine/grand/p
expect Command:
pause 250
send n
expect Command:
pause 250
send ,p
expect Command:
pause 250
send h
expect Help:
pause 250
send
expect Command:
pause 250
send e /etc/x
expect Command:
pause 250
send 9p
expect Command:
pause 250
send 2d
expect Command:
pause 250
send u
expect Command:
pause 250
send x
expect sp:
pause 250
send bug -r
expect Enter type:
pause 250
send typo
expect *
pause 250
send a lost report
expect *
pause 250
send !look
expect *
pause 250
send !quit
drain 3000
